namespace pdg { /**
\fn pdg::Sprite::addAnimationDrawable(function callback, AnimationDrawableOptions options)

\brief choose or create artwork for an animation bone during drawing

Use this overload when the artwork depends on the final animation pose or on
application state at draw time. PDG calls your function synchronously during
rendering. Return a Drawing to display it, or \c null to draw nothing.

If you only need to edit an existing drawing, use the Drawing overload; those
edits appear automatically without a JavaScript callback on each draw.

\param callback A synchronous function called as \c callback(context). It must
return a Drawing or \c null. See Callback context below.
\param options An object selecting the bone, placement, and optional drawing
settings. Both overloads use the same \ref animation_drawable_options "Drawing options".

\return A nonzero unsigned integer identifying the registration. Pass this ID to
setAnimationDrawableEnabled(), removeAnimationDrawable(), or
getAnimationDrawableError().

\note Call enableAnimationPose() successfully before registering the callback.
The sprite's animations must have a fixed hierarchy, as described in the
Drawing overload.

\par Example: show a hand highlight on demand
This example assumes \c sprite already has an enabled animation pose and a
bone named \c hand, as in the Drawing overload's example. Update
\c highlightEnabled from your application's input or update code.

\code{.js}
const highlight = pdg.createDrawing();
highlight.addRect(
    new pdg.Rect(-5, -5, 5, 5),
    new pdg.Attributes().fillColor('yellow').fillOpacity(0.5)
);

let highlightEnabled = true;
const highlightId = sprite.addAnimationDrawable(function(context) {
    const hand = context.getTransform();
    return highlightEnabled && hand.alpha > 0 ? highlight : null;
}, {
    bone: 'hand',
    placement: pdg.animationDraw_AfterAll
});
\endcode

The returned Drawing still uses coordinates relative to the bone. PDG applies
the bone's transform; the callback does not need to reposition the artwork.

\par Callback context
The \c context object exposes these two methods:

| Method | Result |
| ------ | ------ |
| \c getTransform(space) | An owned transform object with \c x, \c y, \c rotation (radians), \c scaleX, \c scaleY, and \c alpha fields for the selected bone. |
| \c copyPose() | An owned snapshot of the final animation pose. |

The optional \c space argument uses one of these numeric constants:
- \c pdg.animationSpace_Local: relative to the bone's parent.
- \c pdg.animationSpace_Rig: relative to the animation rig's origin.
- \c pdg.animationSpace_World (default): in the owning layer's coordinates.

\warning The context expires when the callback returns. Do not save it for
later use. Transform objects and pose snapshots returned by its methods are
independent copies and may be retained.

\par Callback timing
Rendering may run zero or multiple times per animation update. The callback
can inspect the pose and choose, create, or edit artwork, but must not advance
animation or integrate simulation. Keep time-dependent state changes in your
application's update code.

\par Errors and replacement images
Registration uses the same validation rules as the Drawing overload. During
drawing, a thrown exception or an invalid return value disables the registration
and releases its callback. Returning \c undefined, an array, another kind of
object, or a Promise is invalid. Use getAnimationDrawableError() to read the
diagnostic; register a new callback to retry.

For \c pdg.animationDraw_ReplaceSlot, returning \c null or an empty Drawing
successfully hides the named authored image. A failed callback or rendering
failure leaves that image visible. Other authored images are unaffected.

Adding, removing, or enabling registrations inside a callback takes effect after
the current frame. Edits to shared drawing contents are ordinary application
changes and are not rolled back if the callback later fails.

\note Headless builds retain callbacks without invoking them. Use
isAnimationDrawingSupported() to check drawing capability. See the Drawing
overload for bounds, stroke thickness, and rendering behavior shared by both forms.

\sa Sprite::addAnimationDrawable(Drawing, AnimationDrawableOptions)
\sa Sprite::enableAnimationPose()
\sa Sprite::setAnimationDrawableEnabled()
\sa Sprite::removeAnimationDrawable()
\sa Sprite::getAnimationDrawableError()

**/ }
